{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Research a sparse-obstacle combinatorial algorithm: deduplicate blocked coordinates, sort them, and compute each obstacle’s paths from the start minus contributions through earlier obstacles that dominate it; apply the same subtraction to the destination. Precompute factorials/inverse factorials modulo 1,000,000,007 (2N−2 is within range) and use 64-bit modular products. Handle blocked start/destination explicitly. Measure its cost across obstacle counts: the O(M²) method is not guaranteed practical by the stated bound, so do not assume sparsity alone makes it faster.\",\"B\":\"Investigate parallelizing the sparse-obstacle recurrence across target obstacles, with each target summing contributions from its valid predecessors; preserve dependencies between obstacle layers/order. Compare scheduling and memory strategies on the four-core ARM64 target, and include duplicate obstacles and equal-coordinate ordering in correctness checks. Report measured results and worst-case scaling; parallel overhead or quadratic work may erase gains.\",\"C\":\"Explore a compact dense-DP alternative for cases where sparse O(M²) work is unattractive: store only rolling rows and blocked-cell data rather than the source’s N×N vector<bool>, and investigate a correct parallel prefix-scan formulation of each row’s recurrence. Compare against sparse approaches over varying N and M, including memory use and crossover behavior. Retain exact modular results for all valid inputs.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only correctness-checked programs from A/B/C, preserving the input/output contract and general-input correctness. Benchmark each candidate and combinations on the target; do not infer additive speedups. The baseline is valid on 4 cases but measures 0.08067 s mean (score 0.5), so prioritize measured improvement while checking memory use and scaling. Keep the best measured implementation rather than combining ideas without evidence.\",\"analysis\":\"The official task is counting monotone paths around blocked cells. The supplied baseline performs O(N²) work and allocates O(N²) blocked-cell storage despite potentially sparse obstacles. Sparse path-count subtraction can reduce work to O(M²), but the stated bound does not ensure that is affordable; a compact rolling-row dense method provides a distinct alternative. The only automatic measurement is a valid baseline (0.080673 s mean, score 0.5, four correctness cases); there are no candidate measurements, so gains must be established experimentally.\"}"}}
